{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":257833,"duration_api_ms":259221,"ttft_ms":194903,"ttft_stream_ms":1684,"time_to_request_ms":17,"num_turns":2,"result":"{\"verdict\":\"CHANGES_REQUESTED\",\"findings\":[\"F1 [BLOCKING — record identity, not a TC-211 judgment]. The normal sample misquotes its rubric digest. `docs/reviews/2026-09-06-sn036-closure-structured-opus.md` (e04f6844...) states the rubric digest was \\\"copied verbatim from this sitting's FILE manifest line — 81a0de90a0d34ae1181cbfe9de083cc22c52d1fb1ac31f8a — with no reconstructed or completed suffix\\\". The supplied manifest digest for `docs/rubrics/change-review-records.md` is 81a0de90c98ad2dac0b9774fd94c611181fc49bfde083cc22c52d1fb1ac31f8a. The quoted string is 48 hex characters, not 64; its head (81a0de90) and tail (de083cc22c52d1fb1ac31f8a) match, its middle does not, and the interpolated fragment `a0d34ae1` occurs instead in the brief's digest (93d0c2d6a0d34ae1...). The anchors used (G1-G5/B1-B5) match the supplied rubric exactly, so this is transcription corruption of the same file, not a different rubric — but the record's compliance claim is false, and `docs/reviews/2026-09-06-sn036-review-dispositions.md` had explicitly directed \\\"quote the actual rubric SHA256 exactly, with no invented suffix\\\". Fix: replace the string with the 64-char digest, or state the identity as the manifest line without re-typing it. This is the sample record's provenance, so it is not deferrable to WI-607.\",\"F2 [advisory — my own execution residual, independently derived]. SR-161 carries two clauses. I verified the record-side clause both positively and negatively (see basis). The brief-side clause — that the brief presents every applicable question — I verified only on the normal sample: the procedure at digest 90d3a323 supplies no abnormal input in which the brief silently omits an applicable hat's question, so its discriminating power is asserted, not exhibited. I reached this before reading the closure record's A2 and record it as corroboration, not adoption. Adding such an input exceeds this execution's scope; it belongs to the next revision of the procedure.\",\"F3 [observation, non-blocking]. `docs/log.d/2026-09-06-sn036-owner-ruling.md` states \\\"nine of sixteen roster hats on `applies_when = \\\\\\\"always\\\\\\\"`\\\". At `docs/requirements/hats.toml` digest 5d5d1860 the count is ten (SECURITY, MAINTAINER, TEST-ENGINEER, UX-DESIGNER, UX-ENGINEER, ACCESSIBILITY, PERFORMANCE, CONSISTENCY, INTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY, PRODUCT-FITNESS). The error understates the withdrawn cell count, so it strengthens rather than weakens the ruling's conclusion; no amended cell depends on the figure. Correct it if the ruling text is ever re-read as evidence.\",\"F4 [second bounded judgment — contradictory control preflight record]. `CONTROL-PREFLIGHT.md` (9c8d704b) contradicts itself about live provider contact. The header says \\\"No provider request, runner launch, queue mutation, pause change, policy change, smoke run or full run was made for this record\\\", and \\\"Routes and local probes\\\" says \\\"No authentication, account-credit, billing, model-availability or live prompt probe was run, so those properties remain unverified.\\\" The penultimate section then reports \\\"The Claude CLI readiness probe succeeded with initialization and returned model usage both naming `claude-opus-5` ... This verifies that requested review route on this account.\\\" A completed readiness probe returning model usage is a provider request and a model-availability/live-prompt probe. This is the document the owner reads immediately before the irreversible launch act (reviewed deletion of `docs/work/pause`), so the negative claims must be narrowed to \\\"except the tracked readiness probe recorded below\\\" before it is presented for that act.\",\"F5 [second bounded judgment — adjudication queue gap]. `WI-605` ledgers snapshot absorption for `system-requirements.toml` (18 named SR rows) and `low-level-requirements.toml` (35 named LLR rows), and forbids a snapshot \\\"for either affected registry\\\" without judging every absorbed cell. But it also targets a TC-178 `Expected` amendment, which lives in a third registry, `docs/test/test-cases.toml`; that registry has no absorption population, no prohibition and no ledger anywhere in the supplied spec. Under CONTROL-PREFLIGHT's own rule that \\\"a snapshot copies its named registry whole\\\", the one snapshot the WI's targeted amendment most plainly requires is the one it does not govern. Second, the escape route it offers — \\\"a separately authorized per-registry approval reference\\\" — is named in no supplied source, so with 53 ledgered non-authorized rows the only reachable outcome as written is \\\"return the amendment\\\". Both should be resolved before the row is claimable.\",\"F6 [second bounded judgment — advisory]. CONTROL-PREFLIGHT states \\\"returned **16** ready queued WIs\\\" under \\\"Queue observation\\\", then \\\"18 ready WIs ... This supersedes the earlier census above\\\" under \\\"Final candidate preparation\\\", while the \\\"Sources and commands inspected\\\" bullet still reads \\\"all 16 ready WI headers/spec context\\\". The superseded figure survives in the sources list, and the `fig` marker claiming \\\"final working tree\\\" sits under the 16 count. Both are re-run at the frozen revision anyway, so this is clerical, but it leaves two live counts in one read-only record.\",\"F7 [second bounded judgment — advisory]. `WI-607` Done-when 1 directs an agent at `buildtier = \\\"quick\\\"` to edit prose in `docs/requirements/hats.toml`, whose own header declares \\\"THIS FILE IS OWNER TEXT, NOT AGENT TEXT, and it is MARKED FOR THE OWNER'S EDIT AT RETURN\\\". The edit is comment-only and does not touch the three governed fields, but the file's stated ownership rule and the WI's instruction conflict on their face; mark that sub-step owner-confi